window.HU_ESAT=[{"id": "EA1-1", "paper": "EA1", "n": 1, "q": "<p>A tank is a cuboid with a horizontal rectangular base measuring $2\\text{ m}$ by $1.5\\text{ m}$, and it starts empty. Tap $A$ delivers $15$ litres per minute and tap $B$ delivers $9$ litres per minute. A drain at the bottom removes water at a constant $18$ litres per minute whenever it is open and there is water in the tank.<\/p><p>At the start, tap $A$ is turned on, but the drain has been left open by mistake. Ten minutes later tap $B$ is also turned on. When the water first reaches a depth of $12\\text{ cm}$, the drain is closed. How long after the start does the water reach a depth of $60\\text{ cm}$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$5$ hours $10$ minutes", "$2$ hours $15$ minutes", "$2$ hours $10$ minutes", "$2$ hours $20$ minutes", "$2$ hours", "$1$ hour $15$ minutes"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>The base has area $3\\text{ m}^2$, so each centimetre of depth is $0.03\\text{ m}^3 = 30$ litres.<\/p><p>For the first $10$ minutes tap $A$ alone cannot beat the drain ($15 &lt; 18$), so the tank stays empty: the water level cannot go negative. From then on the net rate is $15 + 9 - 18 = 6$ litres per minute, so reaching $12\\text{ cm}$ ($360$ litres) takes $60$ minutes. With the drain closed the rate is $24$ litres per minute, and the remaining $48\\text{ cm}$ ($1440$ litres) takes another $60$ minutes. The total is $10 + 60 + 60 = 130$ minutes.<\/p><p>The trap is to let the tank go to $-30$ litres in the first ten minutes, which adds $5$ minutes and gives $2$ hours $15$ minutes.<\/p>", "tags": ["units", "ratio"], "rating": 6.8}, {"id": "EA1-2", "paper": "EA1", "n": 2, "q": "<p>The times, $t$ minutes, taken by a group of runners to complete a course are shown in a histogram with four bars. The bar for $0\\le t&lt;10$ is $1\\text{ cm}$ high, the bar for $10\\le t&lt;15$ is $3\\text{ cm}$ high, the bar for $15\\le t&lt;20$ is $4\\text{ cm}$ high and the bar for $20\\le t&lt;40$ is $0.75\\text{ cm}$ high. Exactly $30$ runners had times in the interval $10\\le t&lt;15$.<\/p><p>Using linear interpolation, and taking the lower and upper quartiles to be the $\\tfrac{n}{4}$th and $\\tfrac{3n}{4}$th values, where $n$ is the number of runners, estimate the interquartile range in minutes.<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{15}{2}$", "$9$", "$\\dfrac{20}{3}$", "$\\dfrac{35}{3}$", "$10$", "$\\dfrac{25}{3}$"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<p>In a histogram, frequency is proportional to area, not height. The $10\\le t&lt;15$ bar has area $5\\times 3 = 15$ units for $30$ runners, so each unit of area is $2$ runners. The frequencies are $10\\times 1\\times 2 = 20$, $30$, $5\\times 4\\times 2 = 40$ and $20\\times 0.75\\times 2 = 30$, so $n = 120$.<\/p><p>The cumulative frequencies at $t = 10, 15, 20, 40$ are $20, 50, 90, 120$. The lower quartile (the $30$th value) is $10$ runners into the second class: $10 + 5\\times\\tfrac{10}{30} = \\tfrac{35}{3}$. The upper quartile (the $90$th value) is exactly at $t = 20$. So the interquartile range is $20 - \\tfrac{35}{3} = \\tfrac{25}{3}$.<\/p><p>Taking the bar heights as proportional to frequency, or reading off the lower quartile at a class boundary, gives the popular wrong answers.<\/p>", "tags": ["stats"], "rating": 6.8}, {"id": "EA1-3", "paper": "EA1", "n": 3, "q": "<p>For how many real values of $k$ does the equation<\/p>\\[(k-1)x^2 - 4x + (k+2) = 0\\]<p>have exactly one real solution for $x$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$5$", "$4$", "$1$", "$3$", "$2$", "$6$"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>If $k = 1$ the equation is linear, $-4x + 3 = 0$, which has exactly one solution. Otherwise it is a quadratic, with exactly one real solution when the discriminant is zero:<\/p>\\[16 - 4(k-1)(k+2) = 0 \\iff k^2 + k - 6 = 0 \\iff k = -3 \\text{ or } k = 2.\\]<p>So there are $3$ values. The usual answer is $2$, from forgetting that the $x^2$ term can vanish.<\/p>", "tags": ["algebra"], "rating": 6.8}, {"id": "EA1-4", "paper": "EA1", "n": 4, "q": "<p>Find the sum of all real solutions of the equation<\/p>\\[\\left(x^2 - 3x\\right)^2 + \\left(x^2 - 3x\\right) - 6 = 0.\\]", "opts": ["$-3$", "$2$", "$6$", "$3$", "$0$", "$-1$"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>Put $u = x^2 - 3x$. Then $u^2 + u - 6 = 0$, so $u = 2$ or $u = -3$.<\/p><p>The equation $x^2 - 3x = 2$ has discriminant $9 + 8 = 17 > 0$, so two real roots with sum $3$. The equation $x^2 - 3x + 3 = 0$ has discriminant $9 - 12 &lt; 0$, so no real roots. (Equivalently, $x^2 - 3x = \\left(x - \\tfrac32\\right)^2 - \\tfrac94 \\ge -\\tfrac94$, so $u = -3$ is impossible.)<\/p><p>The sum is $3$. Adding the root sums of both quadratics without checking gives $6$.<\/p>", "tags": ["algebra"], "rating": 6.9}, {"id": "EA1-5", "paper": "EA1", "n": 5, "q": "<p>Four people are chosen at random. Assume that each person is equally likely to have been born in any of the $12$ months, independently of the others. What is the probability that their four birth months include exactly three different months?<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{55}{864}$", "$\\dfrac{1}{4}$", "$\\dfrac{55}{144}$", "$\\dfrac{55}{72}$", "$\\dfrac{55}{216}$", "$\\dfrac{41}{96}$"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>Exactly three months means one pair shares a month and the other two people have different months from each other and from the pair. Choose the pair in $\\binom42 = 6$ ways, then the months for the pair and the two others in $12\\times 11\\times 10$ ways.<\/p>\\[P = \\frac{6\\times 12\\times 11\\times 10}{12^4} = \\frac{7920}{20736} = \\frac{55}{144}.\\]<p>Leaving out the factor $6$ gives $\\tfrac{55}{864}$. The option $\\tfrac{41}{96}$ is the probability that at least two share a month, which also includes three or four sharing and two separate pairs.<\/p>", "tags": ["prob"], "rating": 6.9}, {"id": "EA1-6", "paper": "EA1", "n": 6, "q": "<p>The numbers $x = 12$ and $y = 4$ are each given correct to the nearest integer. What is the upper bound of<\/p>\\[\\frac{x+y}{x-y}\\,?\\]", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{16}{7}$", "$\\dfrac{15}{7}$", "$\\dfrac{17}{8}$", "$\\dfrac{16}{9}$", "$\\dfrac{17}{7}$", "$2$"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>Since $x$ and $y$ both appear in the numerator and the denominator, you cannot simply take the largest numerator over the smallest denominator: that would need $x$ at its upper bound and at its lower bound at the same time. Instead write<\/p>\\[\\frac{x+y}{x-y} = 1 + \\frac{2y}{x-y},\\]<p>which increases as $y$ increases and as $x$ decreases. So take $x = 11.5$ and $y = 4.5$, giving $\\dfrac{16}{7}$. The tempting $\\dfrac{17}{7}$ comes from $\\dfrac{12.5+4.5}{11.5-4.5}$, which uses two different values of $x$.<\/p>", "tags": ["number"], "rating": 6.9}, {"id": "EA1-7", "paper": "EA1", "n": 7, "q": "<p>How many of the following eight numbers are factors of $2^{20} - 1$?<\/p>\\[3,\\quad 5,\\quad 7,\\quad 11,\\quad 13,\\quad 25,\\quad 31,\\quad 41\\]", "opts": ["$3$", "$6$", "$5$", "$8$", "$7$", "$4$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>Use the difference of two squares: $2^{20} - 1 = (2^{10} - 1)(2^{10} + 1) = 1023\\times 1025$. Now $1023 = 3\\times 11\\times 31$ and $1025 = 25\\times 41$, so<\/p>\\[2^{20} - 1 = 3\\times 5^2\\times 11\\times 31\\times 41.\\]<p>So $3, 5, 11, 25, 31, 41$ are factors and $7$ and $13$ are not: $6$ in total. Splitting further as $(2^5-1)(2^5+1)(2^{10}+1)$ gives the same factors; it is easy to forget that $25$, not just $5$, divides $1025$.<\/p>", "tags": ["number", "algebra"], "rating": 7.0}, {"id": "EA1-8", "paper": "EA1", "n": 8, "q": "<p>Priya cycles from $P$ to $Q$ and Quentin cycles from $Q$ to $P$ along the same road. They set off at the same time and each rides at a constant speed. They pass each other after $2$ hours, and Priya reaches $Q$ exactly $1$ hour $20$ minutes after they pass. How long does Quentin's whole journey take?<\/p>", "opts": ["$6$ hours", "$4$ hours $40$ minutes", "$3$ hours $20$ minutes", "$3$ hours", "$4$ hours", "$5$ hours"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<p>Let the speeds be $p$ and $q$. After they pass, Priya still has to cover the stretch Quentin rode in the first $2$ hours, namely $2q$, and she does it in $\\tfrac43$ hours: $2q = \\tfrac43 p$, so $q = \\tfrac23 p$.<\/p><p>Quentin must similarly cover Priya's first stretch $2p$ at speed $q$, which takes $\\dfrac{2p}{q} = 3$ hours. His whole journey is $2 + 3 = 5$ hours. (In general the meeting time is the geometric mean of the two times after meeting: $2^2 = \\tfrac43\\times 3$.) The option $3$ hours stops at the time after passing; $3$ hours $20$ minutes is Priya's journey.<\/p>", "tags": ["ratio", "algebra"], "rating": 7.0}, {"id": "EA1-9", "paper": "EA1", "n": 9, "q": "<p>$AB$ is a diameter of a circle, and the tangent to the circle at $B$ is drawn. A straight line through $A$ meets the circle again at $C$ and meets the tangent at $T$, with $C$ between $A$ and $T$. Given that $AC = 9$ and $CT = 16$, find the distance from $C$ to the line $AB$.<\/p>", "opts": ["$12$", "$\\dfrac{48}{5}$", "$\\dfrac{36}{5}$", "$\\dfrac{15}{2}$", "$\\dfrac{27}{5}$", "$9$"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>The tangent is perpendicular to the diameter, so $\\angle ABT = 90^\\circ$, and the angle in a semicircle gives $\\angle ACB = 90^\\circ$. So $BC$ is the altitude from the right angle of triangle $ABT$, whose hypotenuse is $AT = 25$.<\/p><p>Triangles $ACB$ and $ABT$ are similar (common angle $A$, each with a right angle), so $AB^2 = AC\\times AT = 225$ and $AB = 15$; then $BC = \\sqrt{225 - 81} = 12$. In the right-angled triangle $ACB$, the distance from $C$ to $AB$ is the altitude to the hypotenuse:<\/p>\\[h = \\frac{AC\\times BC}{AB} = \\frac{9\\times 12}{15} = \\frac{36}{5}.\\]<p>The options $\\tfrac{27}{5}$ and $\\tfrac{48}{5}$ are the two pieces into which this altitude cuts $AB$.<\/p>", "tags": ["geometry"], "rating": 7.0}, {"id": "EA1-10", "paper": "EA1", "n": 10, "q": "<p>A regular hexagon has area $60\\text{ cm}^2$. Label its sides $1$ to $6$ in order around the hexagon. The midpoints of sides $1$, $3$ and $5$ are joined to form a triangle. What is the area of this triangle in $\\text{cm}^2$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$15$", "$22.5$", "$20$", "$24$", "$30$", "$25$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>Take the hexagon with side $2$, made of six equilateral triangles of side $2$, so its area is $6\\sqrt3$. The midpoint of each side is at distance $\\sqrt3$ from the centre (the height of one of those equilateral triangles), and the three chosen midpoints are $120^\\circ$ apart.<\/p><p>So the triangle is equilateral with circumradius $\\sqrt3$, hence side $\\sqrt3\\times\\sqrt3 = 3$ and area $\\tfrac{\\sqrt3}{4}\\times 9 = \\tfrac{9\\sqrt3}{4}$. The ratio to the hexagon is $\\tfrac{9}{24} = \\tfrac38$, and $\\tfrac38\\times 60 = 22.5$. The option $30$ is the triangle on alternate vertices, which is half the hexagon.<\/p>", "tags": ["geometry"], "rating": 7.0}, {"id": "EA1-11", "paper": "EA1", "n": 11, "q": "<p>What is the smallest positive integer $n$ such that $12n$ is a perfect square and $18n$ is a perfect cube?<\/p>", "opts": ["$36$", "$144$", "$24$", "$72$", "$12$", "$6$"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>Any prime other than $2$ or $3$ in $n$ would only make $n$ bigger, so let $n = 2^a 3^b$. Then $12n = 2^{a+2}3^{b+1}$ is a square when $a$ is even and $b$ is odd, and $18n = 2^{a+1}3^{b+2}$ is a cube when $a\\equiv 2$ and $b\\equiv 1 \\pmod 3$.<\/p><p>The smallest choices are $a = 2$ and $b = 1$, so $n = 12$. Check: $12\\times 12 = 144 = 12^2$ and $18\\times 12 = 216 = 6^3$.<\/p>", "tags": ["number"], "rating": 7.0}, {"id": "EA1-12", "paper": "EA1", "n": 12, "q": "<p>Evaluate<\/p>\\[\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{1}+1\\sqrt{2}} + \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}+2\\sqrt{3}} + \\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{3}+3\\sqrt{4}} + \\cdots + \\frac{1}{100\\sqrt{99}+99\\sqrt{100}}.\\]", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{11}{10}$", "$1$", "$\\dfrac{10}{11}$", "$9$", "$\\dfrac{99}{100}$", "$\\dfrac{9}{10}$"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<p>The general term is $\\dfrac{1}{(k+1)\\sqrt{k} + k\\sqrt{k+1}}$. Take out $\\sqrt{k}\\sqrt{k+1}$ and rationalise:<\/p>\\[\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{k}\\sqrt{k+1}\\left(\\sqrt{k+1}+\\sqrt{k}\\right)} = \\frac{\\sqrt{k+1}-\\sqrt{k}}{\\sqrt{k}\\sqrt{k+1}} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{k}} - \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{k+1}}.\\]<p>Summing from $k = 1$ to $99$ telescopes to $1 - \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{100}} = \\dfrac{9}{10}$. Writing $\\tfrac1k - \\tfrac1{k+1}$ by mistake gives $\\tfrac{99}{100}$.<\/p>", "tags": ["number", "algebra"], "rating": 7.1}, {"id": "EA1-13", "paper": "EA1", "n": 13, "q": "<p>The positive real number $x$ satisfies $x - \\dfrac{1}{x} = 3$. Find the value of<\/p>\\[x^6 - \\frac{1}{x^6}.\\]", "opts": ["$396$", "$120\\sqrt{13}$", "$36\\sqrt{13}$", "$360\\sqrt{13}$", "$-360\\sqrt{13}$", "$1296$"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>Factorise: $x^6 - \\dfrac{1}{x^6} = \\left(x^3 - \\dfrac{1}{x^3}\\right)\\left(x^3 + \\dfrac{1}{x^3}\\right)$.<\/p><p>With $t = x - \\tfrac1x = 3$, cubing gives $x^3 - \\tfrac1{x^3} = t^3 + 3t = 36$. Next, $\\left(x + \\tfrac1x\\right)^2 = t^2 + 4 = 13$, and since $x>0$, $s = x + \\tfrac1x = \\sqrt{13}$. Then $x^3 + \\tfrac1{x^3} = s^3 - 3s = 13\\sqrt{13} - 3\\sqrt{13} = 10\\sqrt{13}$.<\/p><p>So the answer is $36\\times 10\\sqrt{13} = 360\\sqrt{13}$. Ignoring the condition $x>0$ allows the negative value; using $x^3 + \\tfrac1{x^3} = 36$ as well gives $1296$.<\/p>", "tags": ["algebra"], "rating": 7.1}, {"id": "EA1-14", "paper": "EA1", "n": 14, "q": "<p>A triangle has sides of length $5$, $7$ and $8$. What is the radius of the circle inscribed in the triangle?<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$", "$\\sqrt{3}$", "$\\dfrac{5\\sqrt{3}}{4}$", "$2$", "$\\dfrac{7\\sqrt{3}}{3}$", "$2\\sqrt{3}$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>By the cosine rule, the angle $\\theta$ opposite the side $7$ has $\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{25 + 64 - 49}{2\\times 5\\times 8} = \\dfrac12$, so $\\theta = 60^\\circ$ and the area is $\\tfrac12\\times 5\\times 8\\times\\sin 60^\\circ = 10\\sqrt3$.<\/p><p>Splitting the triangle into three triangles from the incentre gives area $= r\\times s$, where $s = 10$ is the semi-perimeter. So $r = \\dfrac{10\\sqrt3}{10} = \\sqrt3$. Using the full perimeter gives $\\tfrac{\\sqrt3}{2}$; the option $\\tfrac{7\\sqrt3}{3}$ is the circumradius.<\/p>", "tags": ["geometry"], "rating": 7.1}, {"id": "EA1-15", "paper": "EA1", "n": 15, "q": "<p>The five numbers $3$, $7$, $12$, $15$ and $x$, where $x$ is a real number, have a mean equal to their median. What is the sum of all possible values of $x$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$21$", "$30$", "$23$", "$\\dfrac{37}{4}$", "$\\dfrac{121}{4}$", "$\\dfrac{129}{4}$"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>The mean is $\\dfrac{37 + x}{5}$. The median depends on where $x$ sits.<\/p><p>If $x\\le 7$ the median is $7$, so $37 + x = 35$ and $x = -2$, which is valid. If $7\\le x\\le 12$ the median is $x$, so $37 + x = 5x$ and $x = \\tfrac{37}{4}$, valid. If $x\\ge 12$ the median is $12$, so $x = 23$, valid.<\/p><p>The sum is $-2 + \\tfrac{37}{4} + 23 = \\tfrac{121}{4}$. Assuming $x$ must be positive (it need not be) loses $-2$ and gives $\\tfrac{129}{4}$.<\/p>", "tags": ["stats"], "rating": 7.2}, {"id": "EA1-16", "paper": "EA1", "n": 16, "q": "<p>A solid wooden cube has edges of length $10\\text{ cm}$. Three square tunnels, each with cross-section $4\\text{ cm}$ by $4\\text{ cm}$, are cut right through the cube. Each tunnel runs between the centres of a pair of opposite faces, with its sides parallel to the edges of the cube. What is the total surface area, inside and outside, of the resulting solid, in $\\text{cm}^2$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$888$", "$984$", "$1080$", "$600$", "$696$", "$792$"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<p>Outside: each face loses a $4\\times 4$ square, leaving $6\\times(100 - 16) = 504$.<\/p><p>Inside: the three tunnels share a central $4\\times 4\\times 4$ cube, all of whose faces are open, so there are no walls there. From each face, a tunnel arm runs $\\tfrac{10 - 4}{2} = 3\\text{ cm}$ in to the central cube, and has four walls of $4\\times 3$, so area $48$. There are $6$ arms, giving $288$.<\/p><p>The total is $504 + 288 = 792$. Treating each tunnel as having full length walls ($3\\times 4\\times 40 = 480$) gives $984$, which counts walls through the hollow centre.<\/p>", "tags": ["geometry"], "rating": 7.3}, {"id": "EA1-17", "paper": "EA1", "n": 17, "q": "<p>A bag contains $4$ red and $3$ blue counters. Counters are taken out one at a time, at random and without replacement, until the bag is empty. What is the probability that the second blue counter is taken out before the third red counter?<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{22}{35}$", "$\\dfrac{3}{7}$", "$\\dfrac{1}{2}$", "$\\dfrac{13}{35}$", "$\\dfrac{4}{7}$", "$\\dfrac{18}{35}$"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>The second blue comes before the third red exactly when at least two of the first four counters are blue (in the first four draws, one of these two events must happen, and they cannot both).<\/p><p>Every set of four counters is equally likely to be the first four, out of $\\binom74 = 35$. All four red: $1$ way. Exactly one blue: $3\\times\\binom43 = 12$ ways. So the probability is $1 - \\tfrac{13}{35} = \\tfrac{22}{35}$.<\/p><p>The option $\\tfrac{13}{35}$ is the complement, and $\\tfrac{18}{35}$ counts only exactly two blues among the first four.<\/p>", "tags": ["prob"], "rating": 7.4}, {"id": "EA1-18", "paper": "EA1", "n": 18, "q": "<p>Three different vertices of a cube are chosen at random, with every set of three vertices equally likely. What is the probability that they form a right-angled triangle?<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{3}{7}$", "$\\dfrac{5}{7}$", "$\\dfrac{4}{7}$", "$\\dfrac{1}{7}$", "$\\dfrac{1}{2}$", "$\\dfrac{6}{7}$"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<p>There are $\\binom83 = 56$ sets, and no three vertices of a cube are collinear. It is easier to count the triangles that are <em>not<\/em> right-angled.<\/p><p>Take a vertex $V$ and its three neighbours: the neighbours are joined to each other by face diagonals, so they form an equilateral triangle. This gives $8$ equilateral triangles, one for each vertex. Every other triangle contains an edge of the cube, and its sides are either $1, 1, \\sqrt2$ (half a face) or $1, \\sqrt2, \\sqrt3$ (an edge, a face diagonal and a space diagonal), taking the edge as $1$. Both satisfy Pythagoras, so all of these are right-angled. So the probability is $\\dfrac{56 - 8}{56} = \\dfrac67$.<\/p><p>Counting only the triangles lying in a face, $6\\times 4 = 24$, gives the trap answer $\\tfrac37$.<\/p>", "tags": ["prob", "geometry"], "rating": 7.4}, {"id": "EA1-19", "paper": "EA1", "n": 19, "q": "<p>Two chords $AC$ and $BD$ of a circle are perpendicular and meet at a point $X$ inside the circle. Given that $AX = 2$, $XC = 6$ and $BX = 3$, find the radius of the circle.<\/p>", "opts": ["$2\\sqrt{5}$", "$\\dfrac{\\sqrt{65}}{2}$", "$4$", "$\\dfrac{7}{2}$", "$\\sqrt{65}$", "$\\dfrac{\\sqrt{61}}{2}$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>Triangles $AXB$ and $DXC$ are similar (angles in the same segment), so $AX\\times XC = BX\\times XD$, giving $XD = \\tfrac{12}{3} = 4$.<\/p><p>Now use coordinates with $X$ at the origin: $A = (-2, 0)$, $C = (6, 0)$, $B = (0, 3)$, $D = (0, -4)$. The centre lies on the perpendicular bisector of $AC$, the line $x = 2$, and on that of $BD$, the line $y = -\\tfrac12$. So the centre is $\\left(2, -\\tfrac12\\right)$ and<\/p>\\[r^2 = (6-2)^2 + \\left(\\tfrac12\\right)^2 = \\tfrac{65}{4}, \\qquad r = \\tfrac{\\sqrt{65}}{2}.\\]<p>This is just over $4$, which is the tempting answer if you assume $AC$ is a diameter.<\/p>", "tags": ["geometry"], "rating": 7.5}, {"id": "EA1-20", "paper": "EA1", "n": 20, "q": "<p>A sealed hollow cone of height $12\\text{ cm}$ contains some water. When the cone stands on its circular base, with its vertex pointing up, the water is $6\\text{ cm}$ deep. The cone is turned over so that it balances with its vertex pointing down and its base horizontal. How deep, in cm, is the water now? (Ignore the thickness of the walls.)<\/p>", "opts": ["$3\\sqrt[3]{7}$", "$6\\sqrt[3]{7}$", "$6$", "$3\\sqrt{14}$", "$6\\sqrt[3]{4}$", "$10.5$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>With the vertex up, the air fills a small cone of height $6$, which is similar to the whole cone with scale factor $\\tfrac12$, so it has $\\tfrac18$ of the volume. The water is therefore $\\tfrac78$ of the cone.<\/p><p>With the vertex down, the water forms a cone of depth $d$ similar to the whole cone, so $\\left(\\dfrac{d}{12}\\right)^3 = \\dfrac78$ and $d = 12\\times\\dfrac{\\sqrt[3]{7}}{2} = 6\\sqrt[3]{7}\\approx 11.5$. Thinking the water fills half the volume gives $6\\sqrt[3]{4}$; using the scale factor linearly gives $10.5$.<\/p>", "tags": ["geometry"], "rating": 7.5}, {"id": "EA1-21", "paper": "EA1", "n": 21, "q": "<p>An escalator moves downwards at a constant speed. Ria walks down it, from top to bottom, while it is moving. When she walks at $1$ step per second she takes $30$ steps to reach the bottom. When she walks at $3$ steps per second she takes $45$ steps. How many steps would she take to walk down the escalator if it were switched off?<\/p>", "opts": ["$45$", "$90$", "$60$", "$75$", "$50$", "$40$"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>Let the escalator have $N$ visible steps and move at $e$ steps per second. At $1$ step per second Ria walks for $30$ seconds, while the escalator carries her $30e$ steps, so $N = 30 + 30e$. At $3$ steps per second she walks for $15$ seconds, so $N = 45 + 15e$.<\/p><p>Equating, $15e = 15$, so $e = 1$ and $N = 60$. The common error is to treat the two trips as taking the same time.<\/p>", "tags": ["ratio", "algebra"], "rating": 7.6}, {"id": "EA1-22", "paper": "EA1", "n": 22, "q": "<p>How many ordered pairs $(x, y)$ of integers satisfy<\/p>\\[xy = 4x + 6y\\,?\\]", "opts": ["$4$", "$8$", "$12$", "$15$", "$24$", "$16$"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<p>Rearrange and factorise: $xy - 4x - 6y + 24 = 24$, so<\/p>\\[(x - 6)(y - 4) = 24.\\]<p>Every way of writing $24$ as a product of two integers $d\\times \\tfrac{24}{d}$ gives exactly one pair, with $x = 6 + d$. The number $24$ has $8$ positive divisors, and $d$ may also be negative, so there are $16$ pairs (including $(0,0)$). Counting only positive divisors gives $8$.<\/p>", "tags": ["algebra", "number"], "rating": 7.7}, {"id": "EA1-23", "paper": "EA1", "n": 23, "q": "<p>Asha and Bilal take turns to roll a fair six-sided die, with Asha going first. Asha wins if she rolls a $6$ on her turn; Bilal wins if he rolls a $5$ or a $6$ on his turn. They keep going until someone wins. What is the probability that Asha wins?<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{1}{3}$", "$\\dfrac{5}{8}$", "$\\dfrac{3}{8}$", "$\\dfrac{6}{11}$", "$\\dfrac{3}{11}$", "$\\dfrac{1}{2}$"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>Let $p$ be the probability that Asha wins. A full round with no winner happens with probability $\\tfrac56\\times\\tfrac46 = \\tfrac59$, after which the game is in its starting position again. So<\/p>\\[p = \\frac16 + \\frac59 p \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\frac49 p = \\frac16 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad p = \\frac38.\\]<p>Going first does not make up for Bilal's doubled chance on each turn. The option $\\tfrac{6}{11}$ is the answer when both need a $6$; $\\tfrac13$ is Asha's share of the single roll chances $\\tfrac16 : \\tfrac26$, which ignores the turn order.<\/p>", "tags": ["prob"], "rating": 7.8}, {"id": "EA1-24", "paper": "EA1", "n": 24, "q": "<p>Triangle $ABC$ has $AB = 13$, $BC = 14$ and $CA = 15$. The bisector of angle $BAC$ meets $BC$ at $E$, and $M$ is the midpoint of $BC$. What is the area of triangle $AEM$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$3$", "$6$", "$\\dfrac{3}{2}$", "$12$", "$\\dfrac{9}{2}$", "$9$"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>Drop the perpendicular $AD$ to $BC$ and let $BD = p$. Then $13^2 - p^2 = 15^2 - (14-p)^2$, giving $p = 5$ and $AD = 12$.<\/p><p>The angle bisector theorem gives $BE : EC = AB : AC = 13 : 15$, so $BE = \\tfrac{13}{28}\\times 14 = 6.5$, while $BM = 7$. Hence $EM = 0.5$, and triangle $AEM$ has base $EM$ on $BC$ and height $AD$:<\/p>\\[\\text{area} = \\tfrac12\\times 0.5\\times 12 = 3.\\]<p>The option $9$ is the area of triangle $ADE$ (with $DE = 1.5$), and $12$ is the area of triangle $ADM$ (with $DM = 2$).<\/p>", "tags": ["geometry"], "rating": 7.9}, {"id": "EA1-25", "paper": "EA1", "n": 25, "q": "<p>$ABCD$ is a square of side $12$, labelled in order. $E$ is the midpoint of $BC$ and $F$ is the midpoint of $CD$. The line segments $AE$ and $BF$ meet at $P$. What is the area of the quadrilateral $APFD$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$76$", "$72$", "$84$", "$\\dfrac{384}{5}$", "$\\dfrac{432}{5}$", "$\\dfrac{396}{5}$"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<p>$APFD$ is what is left of the square after removing the union of triangles $ABE$ and $BCF$. Each has area $\\tfrac12\\times 12\\times 6 = 36$, and they overlap in triangle $BPE$.<\/p><p>Triangles $ABE$ and $BCF$ are congruent, and rotating one onto the other by $90^\\circ$ shows $AE\\perp BF$. So $BPE$ is right-angled at $P$ and similar to $ABE$ (which is right-angled at $B$), with scale factor $\\dfrac{BE}{AE} = \\dfrac{6}{6\\sqrt5} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt5}$. Its area is $\\dfrac{36}{5}$.<\/p>\\[\\text{area of } APFD = 144 - \\left(36 + 36 - \\frac{36}{5}\\right) = 72 + \\frac{36}{5} = \\frac{396}{5}.\\]<p>Forgetting the overlap gives $72$; adding it twice gives $\\tfrac{432}{5}$.<\/p>", "tags": ["geometry"], "rating": 8.0}, {"id": "EA1-26", "paper": "EA1", "n": 26, "q": "<p>A right-angled triangle has hypotenuse of length $10$, and the circle inscribed in it has radius $1$. What is the area of the triangle?<\/p>", "opts": ["$11$", "$\\dfrac{21}{2}$", "$22$", "$10$", "$12$", "$24$"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>Let the legs be $a$ and $b$. The incircle touches the two legs at distance $r = 1$ from the right-angled vertex (the centre and the two touching points form a square), and the two tangents from each other vertex are equal. So the hypotenuse is $(a - 1) + (b - 1)$, giving $a + b = 12$.<\/p><p>Then $2ab = (a+b)^2 - (a^2 + b^2) = 144 - 100 = 44$, so the area is $\\tfrac12 ab = 11$. (The legs are $6\\pm\\sqrt{14}$, so such a triangle exists.) Guessing the familiar $6, 8, 10$ triangle gives $24$, but that triangle has inradius $2$.<\/p>", "tags": ["geometry"], "rating": 8.2}, {"id": "EA1-27", "paper": "EA1", "n": 27, "q": "<p>Two circles, of radii $4$ and $9$, touch each other externally, and both touch a straight line $\\ell$, on the same side of it. A third circle, smaller than both, fits in the gap enclosed by the two circles and $\\ell$: it touches $\\ell$ and touches each of the other two circles externally. What is the radius of the third circle?<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{36}{13}$", "$1$", "$\\dfrac{13}{9}$", "$\\dfrac{36}{25}$", "$\\dfrac{3}{2}$", "$\\dfrac{6}{5}$"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>First find the distance between the points where two touching circles of radii $R$ and $r$ meet a common line. Their centres are $R + r$ apart and at heights $R$ and $r$, so by Pythagoras the horizontal distance is $\\sqrt{(R+r)^2 - (R-r)^2} = 2\\sqrt{Rr}$.<\/p><p>The touching points of the circles of radii $4$ and $9$ are $2\\sqrt{36} = 12$ apart. If the small circle has radius $x$, its touching point lies between them, $2\\sqrt{4x} = 4\\sqrt{x}$ from the first and $2\\sqrt{9x} = 6\\sqrt{x}$ from the second. So<\/p>\\[4\\sqrt{x} + 6\\sqrt{x} = 12 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\sqrt{x} = \\frac65 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad x = \\frac{36}{25}.\\]<p>Stopping at $\\sqrt{x}$ gives $\\tfrac65$; the option $\\tfrac{36}{13} = \\tfrac{4\\times 9}{4+9}$ is a guess at a harmonic mean.<\/p>", "tags": ["geometry"], "rating": 8.6}, {"id": "EA2-1", "paper": "EA2", "n": 1, "q": "<p>What is the coefficient of $x^5$ in the expansion of $(1 + x + x^2)^6(1 - x)^4$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$0$", "$6$", "$-12$", "$-4$", "$12$", "$-8$"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>Since $(1 + x + x^2)(1 - x) = 1 - x^3$, the expression is $(1 - x^3)^4(1 + x + x^2)^2 = \\left(1 - 4x^3 + 6x^6 - \\dots\\right)\\left(1 + 2x + 3x^2 + 2x^3 + x^4\\right)$. The second bracket has no $x^5$ term, so the only contribution is $(-4x^3)(3x^2)$, giving $-12$. Taking the $x^2$ coefficient of $(1 + x + x^2)^2$ to be $2$ gives $-8$, and forgetting the square altogether gives $-4$.<\/p>", "tags": ["seq"], "rating": 6.8}, {"id": "EA2-2", "paper": "EA2", "n": 2, "q": "<p>How many integers $x$ satisfy \\[\\dfrac{x^2 - 5x + 4}{x^2 - 9} \\le \\dfrac{1}{2}\\,?\\]<\/p>", "opts": ["$4$", "$11$", "$6$", "$5$", "$10$", "$9$"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<p>Do not multiply by $x^2 - 9$, whose sign varies. Subtracting $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ gives $\\dfrac{x^2 - 10x + 17}{2(x-3)(x+3)} \\le 0$. The numerator has roots $5 \\pm 2\\sqrt{2}$, roughly $2.17$ and $7.83$. For $-3 &lt; x &lt; 3$ the denominator is negative, so we need the numerator $\\ge 0$: $-3 &lt; x \\le 5 - 2\\sqrt{2}$. For $x > 3$ we need the numerator $\\le 0$: $3 &lt; x \\le 5 + 2\\sqrt{2}$. For $x &lt; -3$ both are positive. The integers are $-2, -1, 0, 1, 2$ and $4, 5, 6, 7$, nine in all. Multiplying through gives $5 - 2\\sqrt{2} \\le x \\le 5 + 2\\sqrt{2}$, which leads to $5$ (or $4$ after removing $x = 3$).<\/p>", "tags": ["alg"], "rating": 6.8}, {"id": "EA2-3", "paper": "EA2", "n": 3, "q": "<p>Water is poured at a constant rate into an empty container in the shape of an inverted right circular cone, with its vertex at the bottom and its axis vertical. When the depth of the water is $4\\text{ cm}$, the depth is increasing at $9\\text{ cm}$ per minute.<\/p><p>How long does it take for the depth to increase from $4\\text{ cm}$ to $8\\text{ cm}$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{28}{27}\\text{ minutes}$", "$\\dfrac{7}{9}\\text{ minutes}$", "$\\dfrac{2}{3}\\text{ minutes}$", "$\\dfrac{4}{9}\\text{ minutes}$", "$\\dfrac{32}{45}\\text{ minutes}$", "$\\dfrac{16}{9}\\text{ minutes}$"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>By similarity the volume at depth $h$ is $V = ah^3$ for a constant $a$. The constant rate is $\\dfrac{dV}{dt} = 3ah^2\\dfrac{dh}{dt} = 3a(16)(9) = 432a$ per minute. The volume needed is $a(8^3 - 4^3) = 448a$, so the time is $\\dfrac{448}{432} = \\dfrac{28}{27}$ minutes. Assuming the level keeps rising at $9\\text{ cm}$ per minute gives $\\dfrac{4}{9}$.<\/p>", "tags": ["diff"], "rating": 6.8}, {"id": "EA2-4", "paper": "EA2", "n": 4, "q": "<p>A ball is dropped from rest from a height of $5\\text{ m}$ above a horizontal floor and first hits the floor after $1$ second. Each time it bounces, it rises to $\\dfrac{9}{16}$ of the height from which it last fell. The time taken to fall from rest through a height $h$ is proportional to $\\sqrt{h}$, and the time taken to rise to a height $h$ equals the time taken to fall from that height.<\/p><p>Ignoring the time spent in contact with the floor, what is the average speed of the ball from the moment it is dropped until it comes to rest?<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{125}{28}\\text{ m s}^{-1}$", "$\\dfrac{80}{49}\\text{ m s}^{-1}$", "$\\dfrac{20}{7}\\text{ m s}^{-1}$", "$\\dfrac{125}{49}\\text{ m s}^{-1}$", "$5\\text{ m s}^{-1}$", "$\\dfrac{25}{7}\\text{ m s}^{-1}$"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>Distance: $5 + 2\\left(5\\cdot\\dfrac{9}{16} + 5\\cdot\\left(\\dfrac{9}{16}\\right)^2 + \\dots\\right) = 5 + 2\\cdot\\dfrac{45/16}{7/16} = 5 + \\dfrac{90}{7} = \\dfrac{125}{7}\\text{ m}$. Heights scale by $\\dfrac{9}{16}$, so times scale by $\\sqrt{9/16} = \\dfrac{3}{4}$, and the time is $1 + 2\\left(\\dfrac{3}{4} + \\dfrac{9}{16} + \\dots\\right) = 1 + 2\\cdot\\dfrac{3/4}{1/4} = 7\\text{ s}$. The average speed is $\\dfrac{125}{49}\\text{ m s}^{-1}$. Using the ratio $\\dfrac{9}{16}$ for the times as well gives a time of $\\dfrac{25}{7}\\text{ s}$ and the tempting answer $5$.<\/p>", "tags": ["seq"], "rating": 6.9}, {"id": "EA2-5", "paper": "EA2", "n": 5, "q": "<p>Which of the following curves can be obtained from the curve $y = \\log_2 x$ by a single transformation, where the transformation is either one translation or one stretch parallel to a coordinate axis?<\/p><ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>$y = \\log_2 (8x)$<\/li><li>$y = 3 + \\log_8 x$<\/li><li>$y = \\log_2 (x^2)$<\/li><\/ol>", "opts": ["II only", "III only", "II and III only", "I and II only", "none of them", "I only", "I, II and III", "I and III only"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<p>I: $\\log_2(8x) = 3 + \\log_2 x$, a translation of $3$ units upwards (it is also a stretch parallel to the $x$-axis with factor $\\dfrac{1}{8}$). II: $y = 3 + \\dfrac{1}{3}\\log_2 x$. A stretch parallel to the $y$-axis keeps the point $(1,0)$, but II passes through $(1,3)$. A translation or a stretch parallel to the $x$-axis gives $y = \\log_2(x-a) + b$, with gradient $\\dfrac{1}{(x-a)\\ln 2}$, which cannot equal the gradient $\\dfrac{1}{3x\\ln 2}$ of II for every $x$. So II needs two transformations. III: $\\log_2(x^2) = 2\\log_2|x|$ is defined for every $x \\neq 0$ and has two branches, while any translation or stretch of $y = \\log_2 x$ is defined only on a half-line. It agrees with the stretch $y = 2\\log_2 x$ only for $x > 0$. So only I works.<\/p>", "tags": ["explog", "graphs"], "rating": 6.9}, {"id": "EA2-6", "paper": "EA2", "n": 6, "q": "<p>What is the sum of all real solutions of the equation \\[\\log_{x-1}(x^2 - 5x + 7) + 2\\log_{x^2 - 5x + 7}(x - 1) = 3\\,?\\]<\/p>", "opts": ["$2$", "$8$", "$4$", "$6$", "$11$", "There are no real solutions."], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>Both bases must be positive and not equal to $1$. As $x^2 - 5x + 7 = \\left(x - \\dfrac{5}{2}\\right)^2 + \\dfrac{3}{4} > 0$, this means $x > 1$, $x \\neq 2$ and $x \\neq 3$. Writing $L = \\log_{x-1}(x^2 - 5x + 7)$, the second term is $\\dfrac{2}{L}$, so $L^2 - 3L + 2 = 0$ and $L = 1$ or $L = 2$. $L = 1$ gives $x^2 - 5x + 7 = x - 1$, so $x = 2$ or $x = 4$, and only $x = 4$ is allowed (check: $\\log_3 3 + 2\\log_3 3 = 3$). $L = 2$ gives $x^2 - 5x + 7 = (x-1)^2$, so $x = 2$, which is not allowed. The only solution is $x = 4$. Forgetting that a base cannot be $1$ gives $6$ or $8$.<\/p>", "tags": ["explog"], "rating": 6.9}, {"id": "EA2-7", "paper": "EA2", "n": 7, "q": "<p>For which real values of $k$ does the equation \\[\\dfrac{x^2 - 4}{x^2 - 1} = k\\] have exactly two distinct real solutions?<\/p>", "opts": ["$k \\le 1$ or $k > 4$", "$1 &lt; k &lt; 4$", "$k &lt; 1$ or $k > 4$", "$k &lt; 1$ or $k \\ge 4$", "$k > 4$", "$k &lt; 1$"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>Rearranging, $x^2(1 - k) = 4 - k$. For $k = 1$ there is no solution. Otherwise $x^2 = \\dfrac{4-k}{1-k}$, which is never equal to $1$, so the excluded values $x = \\pm 1$ never occur. A positive value of $x^2$ gives two solutions, zero gives one and a negative value gives none. The fraction is positive for $k &lt; 1$ or $k > 4$, zero for $k = 4$ (only $x = 0$) and negative for $1 &lt; k &lt; 4$. So there are exactly two solutions when $k &lt; 1$ or $k > 4$. The value $k = 4$ is the local maximum of the curve, touched at the single point $x = 0$.<\/p>", "tags": ["graphs", "alg"], "rating": 6.9}, {"id": "EA2-8", "paper": "EA2", "n": 8, "q": "<p>The function $f$ is defined by $f(x) = x^2 - 6x + 10$ for $x \\ge 3$, and $f^{-1}$ is its inverse function.<\/p><p>What is the sum of all real solutions of the equation $f(x) = f^{-1}(x)$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$5$", "$7$", "$\\dfrac{15 + \\sqrt{5}}{2}$", "$2$", "$12$", "$\\dfrac{5 + \\sqrt{5}}{2}$"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>$f$ is increasing on its domain. If $f(x) = f^{-1}(x) = y$ then $f(y) = x$; if $x &lt; y$ then $f(x) &lt; f(y)$, that is $y &lt; x$, a contradiction, and $x > y$ fails in the same way. So $y = x$ and $f(x) = x$, giving $x^2 - 7x + 10 = 0$, so $x = 2$ or $x = 5$, and only $x = 5$ lies in the domain. Solving $f(f(x)) = x$ instead also produces the roots of $x^2 - 5x + 5 = 0$; the root $\\dfrac{5+\\sqrt{5}}{2} \\approx 3.6$ is in the domain, but there $f(x) = \\dfrac{5-\\sqrt{5}}{2}$, which is not $f^{-1}(x)$ because $f^{-1}$ only takes values $\\ge 3$.<\/p>", "tags": ["alg"], "rating": 6.9}, {"id": "EA2-9", "paper": "EA2", "n": 9, "q": "<p>How many values of $x$ in the interval $0 \\le x \\le 2\\pi$ satisfy $\\sin 5x = \\sin x$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$8$", "$11$", "$10$", "$7$", "$5$", "$9$"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<p>$\\sin 5x = \\sin x$ means $5x = x + 2k\\pi$ or $5x = \\pi - x + 2k\\pi$. The first gives $x = \\dfrac{k\\pi}{2}$: five values $0, \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\pi, \\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}, 2\\pi$. The second gives $x = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6} + \\dfrac{k\\pi}{3}$: six values $\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}, \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}, \\dfrac{7\\pi}{6}, \\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}, \\dfrac{11\\pi}{6}$. The values $\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ and $\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}$ appear in both lists, so there are $5 + 6 - 2 = 9$ solutions. Missing the overlap gives $11$.<\/p>", "tags": ["trig"], "rating": 6.9}, {"id": "EA2-10", "paper": "EA2", "n": 10, "q": "<p>The curve $y = x^2 - 2x$ and the line $y = mx$ enclose a finite region of area $36$. What is the sum of all possible values of the constant $m$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$-4$", "$-8$", "$0$", "$4$", "$12$", "$-2$"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>They meet where $x^2 - (m+2)x = 0$, at $x = 0$ and $x = m + 2$. The area between $y = mx$ and the parabola is $\\left|\\displaystyle\\int_0^{m+2} \\left((m+2)x - x^2\\right) dx\\right| = \\dfrac{|m+2|^3}{6}$. Setting this equal to $36$ gives $|m + 2| = 6$, so $m = 4$ or $m = -8$, with sum $-4$. The negative value is easy to miss, as is the shift by $2$ (which gives $m = \\pm 6$ and sum $0$).<\/p>", "tags": ["int"], "rating": 6.9}, {"id": "EA2-11", "paper": "EA2", "n": 11, "q": "<p>For a real constant $c$, the shortest distance from the point $(0, c)$ to the curve $y = x^2$ is exactly $1$. What is the sum of all possible values of $c$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{1}{4}$", "$-1$", "$1$", "$0$", "$\\dfrac{5}{4}$", "$\\dfrac{9}{4}$"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>The squared distance to $(x, x^2)$ is $x^2 + (x^2 - c)^2 = u^2 + (1 - 2c)u + c^2$, where $u = x^2 \\ge 0$. If $c \\le \\dfrac{1}{2}$ this increases for $u \\ge 0$, so the nearest point is the origin and the distance is $|c|$; with $c \\le \\dfrac{1}{2}$ this gives only $c = -1$. If $c > \\dfrac{1}{2}$ the minimum is at $u = c - \\dfrac{1}{2}$ and equals $c^2 - \\dfrac{(1 - 2c)^2}{4} = c - \\dfrac{1}{4}$, so $c = \\dfrac{5}{4}$. The sum is $\\dfrac{1}{4}$. The tempting $c = 1$ fails: from $(0,1)$ the nearest points are $\\left(\\pm\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}, \\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)$, at distance $\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$.<\/p>", "tags": ["diff", "coord"], "rating": 7.0}, {"id": "EA2-12", "paper": "EA2", "n": 12, "q": "<p>The points $A$ and $B$ have coordinates $(0,0)$ and $(3,0)$. A point $P$, not on the $x$-axis, moves so that $PA = 2PB$.<\/p><p>What is the greatest possible area of triangle $PAB$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$6$", "$3$", "$\\dfrac{9}{2}$", "$\\dfrac{3}{2}$", "$4$", "$2$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>With $P(x,y)$, $x^2 + y^2 = 4\\left((x-3)^2 + y^2\\right)$ simplifies to $x^2 + y^2 - 8x + 12 = 0$, that is $(x-4)^2 + y^2 = 4$, a circle of radius $2$. The base $AB = 3$ is fixed, and the height $|y|$ is at most $2$, reached at $(4, \\pm 2)$. The greatest area is $\\dfrac{1}{2}\\times 3\\times 2 = 3$.<\/p>", "tags": ["coord"], "rating": 7.0}, {"id": "EA2-13", "paper": "EA2", "n": 13, "q": "<p>Let $a = \\log_2 3$, $b = \\log_3 5$ and $c = \\log_5 8$. Which of the following is true?<\/p>", "opts": ["$c > b > a$", "$b > a > c$", "$a > b > c$", "$c > a > b$", "$a > c > b$", "$b > c > a$"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>Compare each with simple fractions. Since $3^2 > 2^3$, $a > \\dfrac{3}{2}$; since $5^2 &lt; 3^3$, $b &lt; \\dfrac{3}{2}$. So $a > b$. Next, $5^3 = 125 > 81 = 3^4$, so $b > \\dfrac{4}{3}$, while $8^3 = 512 &lt; 625 = 5^4$, so $c &lt; \\dfrac{4}{3}$. Hence $a > b > c$.<\/p>", "tags": ["explog"], "rating": 7.1}, {"id": "EA2-14", "paper": "EA2", "n": 14, "q": "<p>What is the smallest positive integer $n$ for which \\[\\sum_{k=1}^{n} \\log_2\\left(\\dfrac{k+2}{k}\\right) > 10\\,?\\]<\/p>", "opts": ["$44$", "$63$", "$1023$", "$31$", "$45$", "$43$"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>The sum is $\\log_2$ of the product $\\dfrac{3}{1}\\cdot\\dfrac{4}{2}\\cdot\\dfrac{5}{3}\\cdots\\dfrac{n+2}{n}$. The numerators are $3, 4, \\dots, n+2$ and the denominators are $1, 2, \\dots, n$, so everything cancels except $\\dfrac{(n+1)(n+2)}{1\\times 2}$. We need $(n+1)(n+2) > 2^{11} = 2048$. Since $44\\times 45 = 1980$ and $45\\times 46 = 2070$, the answer is $n = 44$. Cancelling as if adjacent terms telescoped leaves $n + 2$ and gives $1023$; forgetting the $2$ gives $31$.<\/p>", "tags": ["seq", "explog"], "rating": 7.1}, {"id": "EA2-15", "paper": "EA2", "n": 15, "q": "<p>The curve $y = x^4 - 4x^3 + ax^2$, where $a$ is a real constant, has exactly one stationary point. Which of the following describes all possible values of $a$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$a &lt; \\dfrac{9}{2}$", "$a \\ge \\dfrac{9}{2}$", "$a > \\dfrac{9}{2}$ or $a = 0$", "$a \\ge \\dfrac{9}{2}$ or $a = 0$", "$a > 9$", "$a > \\dfrac{9}{2}$"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<p>$\\dfrac{dy}{dx} = 4x^3 - 12x^2 + 2ax = 2x(2x^2 - 6x + a)$, so $x = 0$ is always a stationary point. There must be no other, so $2x^2 - 6x + a = 0$ must have no root other than $0$. If $36 - 8a &lt; 0$, that is $a > \\dfrac{9}{2}$, it has no roots. If $a = \\dfrac{9}{2}$ it has the repeated root $x = \\dfrac{3}{2}$, which is a stationary point of inflexion, still a stationary point, so there are two. If $a = 0$ the roots are $0$ and $3$, again two stationary points. So $a > \\dfrac{9}{2}$.<\/p>", "tags": ["diff"], "rating": 7.1}, {"id": "EA2-16", "paper": "EA2", "n": 16, "q": "<p>How many pairs $(n, x)$, where $n$ is an integer and $0 \\le x \\le \\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}$, satisfy \\[3\\sin x + 4\\cos x = n\\,?\\]<\/p>", "opts": ["$14$", "$11$", "$12$", "$15$", "$13$", "$16$"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>Write $3\\sin x + 4\\cos x = 5\\sin(x + \\alpha)$, where $\\alpha$ is acute with $\\sin\\alpha = \\dfrac{4}{5}$. As $x$ runs from $0$ to $\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}$, $x + \\alpha$ runs from $\\alpha$ to $\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2} + \\alpha$, so the value starts at $4$, rises to $5$, falls to $-5$ (when $x + \\alpha = \\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}$), then rises to $-3$ at $x = \\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}$. Counting: $n = 5$ once; $n = 4$ twice ($x = 0$ and once on the way down); each of $3, 2, \\dots, -4$ once on the way down, $8$ pairs; $n = -5$ once; then $-4$ and $-3$ again on the way up. The total is $1 + 2 + 8 + 1 + 2 = 14$. Counting each attainable integer once gives $11$.<\/p>", "tags": ["trig"], "rating": 7.2}, {"id": "EA2-17", "paper": "EA2", "n": 17, "q": "<p>What is the total area of the regions enclosed between the curves $y = \\sin x$ and $y = \\sin 2x$ for $0 \\le x \\le \\pi$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{9}{4}$", "$\\dfrac{5}{2}$", "$\\dfrac{1}{2}$", "$3$", "$2$", "$\\dfrac{7}{2}$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>The curves meet where $\\sin x(2\\cos x - 1) = 0$, at $x = 0, \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}, \\pi$. On $\\left[0, \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right]$, $\\sin 2x$ is on top: $\\left[-\\dfrac{1}{2}\\cos 2x + \\cos x\\right]_0^{\\pi/3} = \\dfrac{3}{4} - \\dfrac{1}{2} = \\dfrac{1}{4}$. On $\\left[\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}, \\pi\\right]$, $\\sin x$ is on top: $\\left[-\\cos x + \\dfrac{1}{2}\\cos 2x\\right]_{\\pi/3}^{\\pi} = \\dfrac{3}{2} + \\dfrac{3}{4} = \\dfrac{9}{4}$. The total is $\\dfrac{5}{2}$. Integrating $\\sin x - \\sin 2x$ over the whole interval gives $2$, because the small region is counted as negative.<\/p>", "tags": ["int", "trig"], "rating": 7.2}, {"id": "EA2-18", "paper": "EA2", "n": 18, "q": "<p>Consider the equation $x^2 - kx + k + 3 = 0$, where $k$ is an integer.<\/p><p>What is the sum of all values of $k$ for which the equation has two integer roots? (The two roots are allowed to be equal.)<\/p>", "opts": ["$11$", "$10$", "$7$", "$4$", "$13$", "$8$"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<p>If the roots are $r$ and $s$, then $r + s = k$ and $rs = k + 3$, so $rs - r - s = 3$, that is $(r-1)(s-1) = 4$. The factorisations $1\\times 4$, $2\\times 2$, $(-1)\\times(-4)$, $(-2)\\times(-2)$ give root pairs $\\{2,5\\}$, $\\{3,3\\}$, $\\{0,-3\\}$, $\\{-1,-1\\}$, and so $k = 7, 6, -3, -2$. The sum is $8$. Forgetting negative factors gives $13$; forgetting repeated roots gives $4$.<\/p>", "tags": ["alg"], "rating": 7.4}, {"id": "EA2-19", "paper": "EA2", "n": 19, "q": "<p>Some circles touch both of the lines $y = 0$ and $3y = 4x$, and also pass through the point $(8, 1)$. What is the sum of the radii of all such circles?<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{17}{4}$", "$\\dfrac{104}{9}$", "$\\dfrac{17}{2}$", "$9$", "$17$", "$\\dfrac{65}{4}$"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>The point $(8,1)$ lies inside the angle between the positive $x$-axis and the ray along $(3,4)$. A circle touching both lines cannot cross either of them, so it lies inside this angle and its centre is on the angle bisector. Adding unit vectors, $(1,0) + \\left(\\dfrac{3}{5}, \\dfrac{4}{5}\\right) = \\left(\\dfrac{8}{5}, \\dfrac{4}{5}\\right)$, so the bisector has direction $(2,1)$ and a circle of radius $r$ has centre $(2r, r)$ (check: its distance from $4x - 3y = 0$ is $\\dfrac{|8r - 3r|}{5} = r$). Passing through $(8,1)$ gives $(8 - 2r)^2 + (1 - r)^2 = r^2$, that is $4r^2 - 34r + 65 = 0$. The discriminant $34^2 - 16\\times 65 = 116$ is positive and both roots are positive, so the sum of the radii is $\\dfrac{34}{4} = \\dfrac{17}{2}$. Taking the bisector to have the average gradient $\\dfrac{2}{3}$ gives $\\dfrac{104}{9}$.<\/p>", "tags": ["coord", "trig"], "rating": 7.4}, {"id": "EA2-20", "paper": "EA2", "n": 20, "q": "<p>What is the sum of all real solutions of the equation $\\sqrt{x + 6} = x^2 - 6$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{5 - \\sqrt{21}}{2}$", "$3$", "$0$", "$\\dfrac{5 + \\sqrt{21}}{2}$", "$1$", "$\\dfrac{-1 - \\sqrt{21}}{2}$"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>Let $y = \\sqrt{x + 6} \\ge 0$. Then $y^2 = x + 6$ and $y = x^2 - 6$; subtracting, $y^2 - x^2 = x - y$, so $(y - x)(y + x + 1) = 0$. If $y = x$, then $x^2 - x - 6 = 0$, so $x = 3$ or $x = -2$, and $y = x \\ge 0$ leaves $x = 3$. If $y = -1 - x$, then $x^2 + x - 5 = 0$, so $x = \\dfrac{-1 \\pm \\sqrt{21}}{2}$, and $y \\ge 0$ needs $x \\le -1$, leaving $x = \\dfrac{-1 - \\sqrt{21}}{2}$. The sum is $\\dfrac{5 - \\sqrt{21}}{2}$. Squaring gives the quartic $x^4 - 12x^2 - x + 30 = 0$; all four of its roots are real and add to $0$, but two of them are extraneous.<\/p>", "tags": ["alg"], "rating": 7.4}, {"id": "EA2-21", "paper": "EA2", "n": 21, "q": "<p>Let $f(x) = x^3 - 3x$. How many distinct real solutions does the equation $f(f(x)) = 2$ have?<\/p>", "opts": ["$4$", "$6$", "$7$", "$3$", "$5$", "$9$"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>Let $u = f(x)$. Then $u^3 - 3u - 2 = (u+1)^2(u-2) = 0$, so $u = -1$ or $u = 2$. The graph of $f$ has a local maximum at $(-1, 2)$ and a local minimum at $(1, -2)$. Since $-2 &lt; -1 &lt; 2$, the line $y = -1$ meets the graph three times. The line $y = 2$ touches the graph at the maximum $x = -1$ and crosses it once more at $x = 2$, giving only two distinct solutions. The total is $3 + 2 = 5$. Assuming every horizontal line between turning values gives three solutions leads to $6$, and counting the degree gives $9$.<\/p>", "tags": ["graphs"], "rating": 7.5}, {"id": "EA2-22", "paper": "EA2", "n": 22, "q": "<p>The line $y=mx$, where $m>0$, meets the curve $y=x^3-6x^2+9x$ at three distinct points, and the line and the curve enclose two regions of equal area. Find $m$.<\/p>", "opts": ["$2$", "$1$", "$\\dfrac94$", "$3$", "$4$", "$\\dfrac12$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>No integration is needed. Every cubic is symmetric under a half turn about its point of inflection. Here $y''=6x-12=0$ at $x=2$, where $y=8-24+18=2$, so the half turn about $(2,2)$ maps the curve to itself. It also maps any line through $(2,2)$ to itself, so such a line cuts off two congruent regions, one on each side of $(2,2)$. The line through the origin and $(2,2)$ is $y=x$, so $m=1$. Check: $x^3-6x^2+9x=x$ gives $x(x-2)(x-4)=0$, three distinct points placed symmetrically about $x=2$. It is the only such line: for $0&lt;m&lt;9$ the other intersections are $x=3\\pm\\sqrt m$, and making the signed area from $0$ to $b=3+\\sqrt m$ vanish gives $b^2-8b+18-2m=0$, which with $m=(b-3)^2$ becomes $b(b-4)=0$, so $b=4$ and $m=1$; for $m>9$ the region to the left of the origin is always much the smaller. Working out one area and halving, or using the tangent gradient at the origin ($9$), leads to the wrong options.<\/p>", "tags": ["int", "graphs"], "rating": 7.5}, {"id": "EA2-23", "paper": "EA2", "n": 23, "q": "<p>What is the sum of all values of $x$ in the interval $0 \\le x \\le \\pi$ that satisfy \\[\\sin x + \\sin 2x + \\sin 3x = \\cos x + \\cos 2x + \\cos 3x\\,?\\]<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{3\\pi}{4}$", "$\\dfrac{11\\pi}{12}$", "$\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}$", "$\\dfrac{17\\pi}{12}$", "$\\dfrac{19\\pi}{24}$", "$\\dfrac{61\\pi}{24}$"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>Expanding $\\sin(2x \\pm x)$ and $\\cos(2x \\pm x)$ gives $\\sin x + \\sin 3x = 2\\sin 2x\\cos x$ and $\\cos x + \\cos 3x = 2\\cos 2x\\cos x$. So the equation is $(1 + 2\\cos x)(\\sin 2x - \\cos 2x) = 0$. Either $\\cos x = -\\dfrac{1}{2}$, giving $x = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}$, or $\\tan 2x = 1$ with $0 \\le 2x \\le 2\\pi$, giving $x = \\dfrac{\\pi}{8}, \\dfrac{5\\pi}{8}$. The sum is $\\dfrac{17\\pi}{12}$. Cancelling the common factor $1 + 2\\cos x$ loses $\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}$ and gives $\\dfrac{3\\pi}{4}$.<\/p>", "tags": ["trig"], "rating": 7.6}, {"id": "EA2-24", "paper": "EA2", "n": 24, "q": "<p>What is the value of \\[\\binom{10}{0} + \\dfrac{1}{2}\\binom{10}{1} + \\dfrac{1}{3}\\binom{10}{2} + \\dfrac{1}{4}\\binom{10}{3} + \\dots + \\dfrac{1}{11}\\binom{10}{10}\\,?\\]<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{1024}{11}$", "$\\dfrac{1023}{11}$", "$\\dfrac{2047}{10}$", "$\\dfrac{2048}{11}$", "$\\dfrac{2047}{11}$", "$\\dfrac{1023}{10}$"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>Integrate the binomial expansion $(1+x)^{10} = \\displaystyle\\sum_{k=0}^{10} \\binom{10}{k}x^k$ from $0$ to $1$. Each term gives $\\dfrac{1}{k+1}\\binom{10}{k}$, so the sum is $\\displaystyle\\int_0^1 (1+x)^{10}\\,dx = \\left[\\dfrac{(1+x)^{11}}{11}\\right]_0^1 = \\dfrac{2^{11} - 1}{11} = \\dfrac{2047}{11}$. Forgetting the lower limit gives $\\dfrac{2048}{11}$.<\/p>", "tags": ["seq", "int"], "rating": 7.8}, {"id": "EA2-25", "paper": "EA2", "n": 25, "q": "<p>For which positive values of $k$ does the equation $e^x = kx^2$ have exactly three real solutions?<\/p>", "opts": ["$0 &lt; k &lt; \\dfrac{e^2}{4}$", "$k > \\dfrac{e}{2}$", "$k \\ge \\dfrac{e^2}{4}$", "$k > \\dfrac{e^2}{2}$", "$k > \\dfrac{e^2}{4}$", "$k > e$"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>Note $x = 0$ is never a solution. For $x &lt; 0$, $g(x) = e^x - kx^2$ has $g'(x) = e^x - 2kx > 0$, with $g(0) = 1 > 0$ and $g(x) \\to -\\infty$ as $x \\to -\\infty$, so there is exactly one negative solution for every $k > 0$. For $x > 0$, the equation is $h(x) = \\dfrac{e^x}{x^2} = k$, and $h'(x) = \\dfrac{e^x(x - 2)}{x^3}$. So $h$ falls from $+\\infty$ to its minimum $h(2) = \\dfrac{e^2}{4}$ and then rises to $+\\infty$, giving two positive solutions when $k > \\dfrac{e^2}{4}$ and one when $k = \\dfrac{e^2}{4}$. Exactly three solutions occur for $k > \\dfrac{e^2}{4}$. The tempting $k \\ge \\dfrac{e^2}{4}$ fails because at $k = \\dfrac{e^2}{4}$ the curves only touch at $x = 2$, giving two solutions in total.<\/p>", "tags": ["explog", "graphs"], "rating": 7.8}, {"id": "EA2-26", "paper": "EA2", "n": 26, "q": "<p>The circles $x^2 + y^2 = 1$ and $(x-4)^2 + y^2 = 4$ have exactly four common tangents. What is the product of the gradients of these four lines?<\/p>", "opts": ["$-\\dfrac{3}{35}$", "$-\\dfrac{9}{7}$", "$\\dfrac{9}{35}$", "$-\\dfrac{1}{15}$", "$\\dfrac{1}{105}$", "$\\dfrac{3}{35}$"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<p>No vertical line touches both circles, so each common tangent is $y = mx + c$. Tangency to the first circle gives $c^2 = 1 + m^2$, and to the second gives $(4m + c)^2 = 4(1 + m^2) = 4c^2$. So $4m + c = \\pm 2c$, that is $c = 4m$ or $c = -\\dfrac{4m}{3}$. Then $16m^2 = 1 + m^2$ gives $m^2 = \\dfrac{1}{15}$, and $\\dfrac{16m^2}{9} = 1 + m^2$ gives $m^2 = \\dfrac{9}{7}$. The gradients are $\\pm\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{15}}$ and $\\pm\\dfrac{3}{\\sqrt{7}}$, with product $\\left(-\\dfrac{1}{15}\\right)\\left(-\\dfrac{9}{7}\\right) = \\dfrac{3}{35}$.<\/p>", "tags": ["coord"], "rating": 8.2}, {"id": "EA2-27", "paper": "EA2", "n": 27, "q": "<p>For how many integer values of $k$ do exactly three distinct tangents to the curve $y = x^3 - x$ pass through the point $(2, k)$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$5$", "$3$", "$9$", "$8$", "$7$", "$6$"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>The tangent at $x = t$ is $y = (3t^2 - 1)(x - t) + t^3 - t = (3t^2 - 1)x - 2t^3$. It passes through $(2,k)$ when $p(t) = 2t^3 - 6t^2 + 2 + k = 0$. A line cannot touch a cubic at two different points, so distinct values of $t$ give distinct tangents, and we need three distinct real roots. As $p'(t) = 6t(t - 2)$, this happens when the local maximum $p(0) = 2 + k$ is positive and the local minimum $p(2) = k - 6$ is negative, so $-2 &lt; k &lt; 6$. That is $7$ integers. At $k = -2$ and $k = 6$ (the point $(2,6)$ is on the curve) there are only two tangents.<\/p>", "tags": ["diff", "graphs"], "rating": 8.6}];
